<body>
<h1>Unit 12: Rate of Heat Flow</h1><p class="meta">Physics XI  |  NEB (Grade 11)  |  Phy. 101 &nbsp;•&nbsp; Content area: Heat &amp; Thermodynamics</p><p class="summary">How fast energy moves as heat depends on the mechanism: contact, bulk motion of a fluid, or electromagnetic radiation. Each mechanism has its own law, its own geometry, and its own place where the textbook approximation fails.</p><h2>What the model asserts</h2><ol><li>Conduction transfers energy through a medium without moving the medium; convection moves bulk fluid; radiation needs no medium at all.</li><li>Fourier's law states dQ/dt = -k A dT/dx, with thermal conductivity k a material property and the minus sign meaning heat flows down the temperature gradient.</li><li>Newton's law of cooling gives the convective rate dQ/dt = h A (T_surface - T_fluid), with h the convection coefficient, which depends on flow conditions rather than on geometry alone.</li><li>The Stefan-Boltzmann law for a blackbody is P = epsilon sigma A T^4, with sigma = 5.670374419e-8 W/(m^2.K^4) exactly in the 2019 SI.</li><li>Thermal resistances add in series and parallel exactly like electrical resistances, with R = L/(kA) for a slab.</li><li>Building heat loss, the thermal performance of a wall, and the ability of a device to shed heat are all set by this network of resistances.</li><li>Conduction, convection and radiation are three separate mechanisms: conduction is the diffusion of particle energy through a medium, convection needs a moving fluid, and radiation needs no medium at all.</li></ol><h2>Conditions, assumptions and limits</h2><ul><li>Fourier's law in its simple slab form assumes steady or quasi-steady state with a one-dimensional gradient; fast transients with a sharp thermal front need the heat equation instead.</li><li>At nanometre gaps, interfacial (Kapitza) resistance dominates, so the bulk conductivity alone badly under-predicts the thermal resistance of a nanostructure.</li><li>Radiation dominates above a few hundred degrees Celsius and in vacuum, so insulation design that ignores it fails in exactly the conditions where it matters most.</li><li>The Stefan-Boltzmann law as written is for a blackbody; real surfaces need an emissivity, and a non-grey surface needs a spectral integral rather than a single epsilon.</li><li>Newton's law of cooling assumes convective loss to air near ambient temperature; at high temperature radiative loss dominates and the cooling curve changes shape.</li></ul><h2>Key numbers</h2><table><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>Stefan-Boltzmann constant (exact)</td><td>5.670374419e-8 W/(m^2.K^4)</td></tr><tr><td>Thermal conductivity of copper</td><td>about 400 W/(m.K)</td></tr><tr><td>Thermal conductivity of still air</td><td>about 0.026 W/(m.K)</td></tr><tr><td>Thermal resistance of 20 cm of brick (k = 0.7 W/(m.K))</td><td>about 0.29 K/W for 1 m^2</td></tr></table><h2>Open questions and mysteries</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>How is heat carried across a single atomic layer - by phonons, by electrons, or by something else?</b></p><p class="status">Frontier</p><p>Ballistic, diffusive and hydrodynamic transport regimes have all been observed in graphene and other two-dimensional materials, and the boundaries between them are still being mapped.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>How much cooling power will future quantum processors demand, and what will set the limit?</b></p><p class="status">Engineering frontier</p><p>Cooling power rises steeply below about 20 K, and the practical limits of cryogenic infrastructure are an active engineering constraint.</p></div><h2>Inquiry prompts</h2><ol><li>Calculate the heat loss through a wall, then repeat for a double-glazed design, and set out the thermal resistance network explicitly.</li><li>Explain why a metal spoon and a wooden spoon feel different although both conduct heat.</li><li>Design a method for measuring the emissivity of a dark surface using a lamp and a thermometer, and estimate its uncertainty.</li></ol><h2>Further reading</h2><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/index">OpenStax: University Physics Volume 2</a></li><li><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/mission/webb/webbs-mirrors/">NASA: Webb's Mirrors</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/1-6-mechanisms-of-heat-transfer">OpenStax: Mechanisms of Heat Transfer</a></li></ul><p class="note">Physics XI — Academic Companion — a companion brief, not a replacement for the prescribed NEB text. The open questions describe live research frontiers; they are enrichment, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed text and syllabus for examinable scope.</p>
